Number = {8},
Pages = {859-865},
Note = {13th International Symposium on Environmental Pollution and Its Impact
   on Life in the Mediterranean Region, Thessaloniki, GREECE, OCT 08-12,
   2005},
Organization = {Mediterranean Sci Assoc Environm Protect},
Abstract = {Particulate matter and its chemical and physical characteristics were
   reviewed for several sites and cities in Greece, and presented in this
   report in the frame of the EU project COST 633. It is the first part of
   the reviewed data on atmospheric aerosols in Greece.
   Collected data refer mostly to PM10 aerosols, since there are not many
   available data for PM2.5 in Greece. PM10 concentrations, at most of the
   sites examined for the cities of Thessaloniki, Athens and Kozani,
   exceeded the EC annual limit of 40 mu g/m(3) that had to be met since
   2005. Black Smoke (BS) concentrations exhibited a significant decrease
   throughout 1984-2001, reflecting the effectiveness of applied policy
   measures.
   In Thessaloniki and Athens, Pb, Cd, V and Mn exhibited quite the same
   levels, in compliance with the existing air quality guidelines.
   SO4= was found to be the predominant ion in fine aerosols. The sulfate
   relationship with the wind conditions implied that in the atmosphere
   they originate from oxidation processes as well as transportation.},
