   Antonio, TX, SEP 29-OCT 02, 2005},
Organization = {Assoc Consumer Res},
Abstract = {Affective misforecasting (AMF) is defined as the gap between predicted
   and experienced affect. Based on prior research that examines AMF, the
   current study uses qualitative and quantitative data to examine the
   sources of AMF (i.e., why it occurs) in the consumption domain. The
   authors find evidence supporting some sources of AMF identified in the
   psychology literature, develop a fuller understanding of others, and,
   find evidence for novel sources of AMF not previously explored.
   Importantly, they find considerable differences in the sources of AMF
   depending on whether feelings are worse than or better than forecast.},
