   INTERDISCIPLINARY TASK, VOLS 1-3},
Year = {2002},
Pages = {1267-1274},
Note = {30th IAHS World Congress on Housing, UNIV COIMBRA, COIMBRA, PORTUGAL,
   SEP 09-13, 2002},
Organization = {Int Assoc Housing Sci},
Abstract = {The paper presents findings about the role of service quality as a
   driver of pre-purchase expectations. Here, pre-purchase expectations are
   acknowledged as being an important basis used by customers to evaluate
   satisfaction. Service quality is seen as a potential input to
   pre-purchase expectations. The orientation of the study is towards
   Australian residential construction. Within this market, the study
   targets a specific type of customer who can be described as: a
   householder; an owner occupier and; an irregular purchaser of
   construction services. Such customers are often directly involved in the
   design and construction process but are also technically inexperienced
   due to their irregular use of the industry. Findings from the study,
   indicate that one sought of customer places a critical pre-purchase
   emphasis on price and product, while others emphasise service quality.
   It appears that the latter occurs as the need for a customised end
   product increases.},
